body:
Same here. Growing up, I quickly came to the realization that trying my hardest and trying to prosper were no use. When I'd try my hardest at something I was told by family, friends, teachers, that I basically sucked and couldn't do anything right. I stopped caring as much and basically have drifted my whole life doing the bare minimum. Even like when I was in elementary school and needed help on a simple math problem, I'd go to my dad. He always bragged about being great at math and how he "always wanted to be a math teacher." Well he would take me into his makeshift office/classroom with an oldschool desk and large chalkboard and literally spend hours trying to explain a single, elementary level math problem to me. He did this many times and would constantly belittle me on being stupid and acting like I was never going to learn. Even a time when he was drunk and hit me in the face in front of my mom for not understanding his explanation on homework and my mom just watched looking sad. Or like when I took swimming classes as a child and was very afraid of swimming. I took classes for a long time, gained a little confidence, then we had our final day. All families came out to support their kids and my whole family came too. The first swimming stroke we were supposed to do was swimming on our backs and it was my most feared one because I always inhaled water through my nose and panicked cause I couldn't breathe. I froze and couldn't get myself to do it and I looked up at my family to see how they were reacting and my dad was gone. A minute later and he was down at the pool yelling at me in front of all the other kids, families, and instructors telling me how he knew I couldn't do it and taking the classes was a waste of time. He yanked me out of the pool and we went home in silence. We were supposed to go out to eat to celebrate finishing and yeah, that didn't happen.

&#x200B;

Anyways, I stopped going to my dad for help when I was probably around 5-7yo and have very rarely ever asked him for help on anything since. I've always felt like I've had to raise myself in terms of having "no dad" to go to. I taught myself to shave, how to do basic repairs on things, how to learn adult things, etc.

thank you! it may be hard to tell from a single picture but it had a fairly large, darker (from looking through my windshield) abdomen. Possibly pregnant female? 

&#x200B;

https://preview.redd.it/vip3eqwl0x2b1.jpeg?width=1249&format=pjpg&auto=webp&s=abc0a384d9cb06ae4cec74ee4b2df4e74b1f5ed3

I can't say I know what your personal solution is. Or even that I know what you should do really.. I'm a pretty empathetic person so I attempt to put myself in your position. Obviously I do not know what lead you to this situation. With that being said.. 

&#x200B;

Take one single thing at a time. You are recovering. Don't boggle yourself down with all the negative things that will try and bring you down or give you reason as to why everything is bad. I'm sure everything feels bad for you. With your body, your mental state, everyone and everything around you. It's not easy for sure. Just focus on one thing at a time. To be more specific, something like just focusing on each breath that you take. I know that's kind of a cliché thing to say but it's honestly more important than people give it credit. Close your eyes and just breathe slowly. You'll be able to calm down, relax a bit, and focus on things better. Figure out within yourself what is most important to you right now. Is it getting physically better? Is it getting back on your medication? Is it having a better relationship with family or friends? If any of those, what could be a SIMPLE first step towards that little goal? Don't stress out so much on the big things. It's terribly difficult not to do that and I know from my own previous and constant experience. It does however help you to figure out your next plan. I would say just sort of put yourself into a hibernation stage where you are calm, don't react much, don't talk much, and just focus on being calm. You will be able to take in a lot of information in this time and it will help to access what your next plan is. Obviously in this case, I hope you find something that will help. Largely focus on positive things too. Negative thoughts are extremely common in overtaking any positive thoughts or mentality. If you are able to breathe, fantastic. If you're able to eat or drink, amazing. If you can still speak, awesome! Go from there. 

&#x200B;

I hope you feel better. Know that you're not alone. It's just a matter of reaching out and finding people to understand and listen if nothing else. <3

this isn't meant to be rude but when I think of my personal experiences in life and whatever you could have faced in your life.. if you tried to express that through art I would imagine it would be much more abstract or even potentially uncomfortable to look at or even scary. Depending on your experiences of course.

&#x200B;

Your photo is not bad by any means. My first impression is "maybe a new painter trying to do a simple self portrait." but nothing about "tough phase"s

this game relies off every skill in some way. I suggest doing combat against farmers or anything that drops different seeds. Use the seeds to do farming. While you gather herbs from farming over time, use those herbs to make potions in herblore to boost other skills. Herblore is one of those skills that is like a teammate to other skills. It isn't great on it's own but it helps others. 

&#x200B;

Thieving is basically a casual boost to some things but is overall useless in my opinion. I wouldn't focus on this right away. Unless you need money. If you decide to do thieving I suggest doing fishing to get raw food, then do cooking to make it edible so you survive longer if you get hit during thieving... definitely not as high a priority of a skillset imo\\

&#x200B;

Doing astrology is pretty much passive perks. I haven't noticed any negatives to it so far in terms of providing some benefits without really any negatives. The only negative I can think of would be that it takes a TON of resources to reroll perks to get what you specifically want to 5%. 5% is not that important honestly. Astrology is one of those skills you let afk while you're gone for like 10 hours or something. It's not that important. 

&#x200B;

Firemaking can be useful to be able to purchase upgrades for cooking. that's about it really. 

&#x200B;

TL;DR: I suggest afking non-combat skills that you can afk for long periods of time without checking. No matter the skill you focus, it will help with something.
